Venetium 0013 - X86-1: two diagnostics that only the rt2.1 toolchain raises, fixed at the narrowest source point.

The first full x86 build with rt2.1 (BATCH-X86-1 section 2) failed on two files that compile with the toolchains Chromium 150 pins.
Both are new, intended diagnostics in the newer compilers, not toolchain bugs.

A. third_party/webrtc/modules/audio_processing/agc2/rnn_vad/rnn_fc.cc - GetActivationFunction() returns a bare function name, so
   webrtc::FunctionView's constructor deduces F = float(&)(float) and tests a function reference for null (api/function_view.h:80).
   Clang 23.1 (llvm-project PR #204944, "warn on a function reference compared/converted to null", fb1de34e, 2026-07-06) diagnoses
   that: -Wpointer-bool-conversion, an error under /WX. Chromium's pinned clang (llvmorg-23-init-10931-g20b6ec66) predates it.
   Fix: return the functions' addresses, so F is a function pointer - same behaviour, no diagnostic. Only rnn_fc.obj recompiles.
   Upstream fixed the header instead (WebRTC CL 488020, api/function_view.h); that would recompile the 367 objects that include it,
   so it is left for the next WebRTC roll. rnn_fc.cc is the only place in chrome's graph that builds a FunctionView from a function.

B. third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/vendor/qr_code-v2/src/structured.rs - u8::max_value() is fully deprecated since Rust 1.99
   (rust-lang/rust#146882: warn-by-default `deprecated`, previously allow-by-default `deprecated_in_future`); the crate's own
   #![deny(warnings)] turns it into an error, which Chromium's -Awarnings / allow_all_warnings.rsp cannot override. rt2.1's rustc is
   1.100.0-nightly (bba531001, 2026-09-20); Chromium 150 pins an older rustc. Fix: u8::MAX (the same value). Only qr_code rebuilds.
   It is the only vendored crate in chrome's graph that combines a deny(warnings) with a newly deprecated API.

Research and two adversarial checks per issue: F:\cr\x86-1\toolchain-research\ (clang.md, rust.md). His decision (2026-10-02):
patch the source for both, as 0013; if that does not help, hand the toolchain issue to rt2.1. Made after section 2's first pass.

